# Vercel's CLI uploads the directory it is invoked from. Since the project's Root Directory is
# `frontend`, a deploy MUST be launched from the repository root (from inside frontend/ Vercel looks
# for frontend/frontend and fails with "No Next.js version detected") — and that means the upload
# would otherwise carry the FastAPI backend, the Android application, the whole docs tree and the
# CodeGraph index along with it. On 2026-08-23 that came back as:
#
#     Error: Request body too large. Limit: 10mb
#
# so the deploy did not fail on anything wrong with the build — it never reached one.
#
# DO NOT REWRITE THIS AS AN ALLOWLIST. The obvious `/*` followed by `!/frontend/` is the standard
# gitignore recipe and it is what I wrote first; Vercel's matcher does not honour the re-include, and
# the deploy died on "The specified Root Directory "frontend" does not exist" — the build never saw
# the one directory the whole payload was for. A denylist does go stale when someone adds a heavy
# top-level directory, so if a deploy starts failing on the 10 MB limit again, the fix is to name the
# new directory here, not to reach for the negation form a second time.
#
# NOTE: THE CLI NEVER READS .gitignore, WITH OR WITHOUT THIS FILE. Until 2026-10-09 this note said
# the file's presence "stops" the CLI consulting it, which implied that deleting the file would bring
# .gitignore back; it would not. Vercel CLI 59.5.0 builds its upload ignore set from a built-in list
# plus this file (or a `.nowignore`) and nothing else, so build and dev output has to be named below
# even though git ignores it. Without this file only the built-in list applies (`.git`, `.vercel`,
# `.next`, `node_modules`, `.env.local`, `.env.*.local` and a few more): not `.env`, `.env.vercel`,
# `.env.e2e` or `*.p12`, and none of the directories below.
#
# Verified 2026-08-23: `next build` under Root Directory `frontend` reads nothing outside frontend/ —
# frontend/vercel.json is the config Vercel picks up, and neither next.config.ts, tsconfig.json nor
# frontend/package.json references a parent path. The root package.json only holds the two node
# scripts (keep-alive, vercel-ci-setup), neither of which runs at build time.

# The other three products in this repository. Nothing here is read by `next build`.
/backend/
/android/
/docker/
/infra/

# Prose, tooling and agent configuration.
/docs/
/scripts/
/.github/
/.claude/
/.agents/
*.docx
/SESSION_HANDOVER.md
/README.md

# Generated locally, never an input to a build.
/.codegraph/
/.ruff_cache/
/test-results/
/node_modules/
/frontend/node_modules/
/frontend/.next/
/frontend/out/
/frontend/coverage/
/frontend/playwright-report/
/frontend/test-results/

# Secrets never travel in a deployment payload; Vercel supplies the environment itself.
#
# ANCHORED AND NARROW ON PURPOSE. This read `.env` + `.env.*`, and a bare `.env.*` matches a BASENAME
# at any depth — so it also excluded `frontend/.env.example`, a committed template with no secret in
# it. The CLI deploy did not mind; `vercel deploy --prebuilt`, which is what CI runs, failed on
# Vercel's side with `ENOENT: no such file or directory, lstat '/vercel/path0/frontend/.env.example'`
# after uploading the whole build. Excluding a tracked file that the deployment's own file list still
# names is not the same as not having it.
/.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# 2026-10-09: what the three lines above let through. They stopped at `/.env`, and the root of a
# working checkout holds more than that: `.env.vercel` (a Vercel token), `.env.e2e` (an end-to-end
# login and password) and `designrepo-release.p12`, the Android release keystore. All three are
# gitignored, but the CLI never reads .gitignore (see the NOTE at the top), so a manual non-prebuilt
# `vercel deploy` from the root would upload them as source files of a deployment that everybody in
# the team can read for as long as it exists.
#
# `/.env.*` IS ANCHORED, which is the whole lesson of the paragraph above. It matches root files
# only, so `frontend/.env.example` and `frontend/.env.local.example` still travel. Checked against
# the source of Vercel CLI 59.5.0 and its bundled @vercel/next 6.0.0 on 2026-10-09: the Next.js
# builder attaches every `.env` and `.env.*` file in the ROOT DIRECTORY (frontend/) to every
# function, which is how a file nobody reads ended up in a function's `filePathMap`. On a `--prebuilt`
# deploy, a map entry this file matches is not uploaded, and Vercel then fails on the missing file.
# It reads no other directory for them, so nothing a function references lives at the repository
# root. The key-material patterns are unanchored on purpose: a keystore or a private key is never a
# build input, wherever it sits. None of them matches a file under frontend/ outside node_modules/,
# and a map entry under node_modules/ is uploaded whatever this file says.
/.env.*
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.pem
